<article class="mx-auto max-w-4xl py-12 sm:py-16">
  <header>
    <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-3">
      <p class="text-sm font-semibold uppercase tracking-widest text-indigo-700">
        Our theory of change
      </p>
      <button
        type="button"
        data-copy-current-url
        data-copy-url={~p"/theory-of-change"}
        data-copy-success-label="Link copied"
        class="rounded-lg border border-gray-300 px-3 py-2 text-sm font-medium hover:bg-gray-50"
      >
        Share this page
      </button>
    </div>
    <h1 class="mt-5 text-4xl font-bold tracking-tight text-gray-900 sm:text-5xl">
      From shared evidence to better democracy
    </h1>
    <p class="mt-6 text-xl leading-8 text-gray-600">
      Can shared evidence and informed participation improve public debate, help decision-makers make better choices, and help new trusted voices emerge?
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      That is the working hypothesis behind YouCongress. Our mission is to improve democracy, starting with AI safety, governance, and the impact of AI on jobs. We are building and testing parts of this process. The path from a useful platform to better democratic outcomes is an ambition, not an impact we have demonstrated.
    </p>
  </header>

  <section aria-labelledby="change-path" class="py-10">
    <h2 id="change-path" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">The path we want to test</h2>
    <ol class="mt-6 space-y-3">
      <li
        :for={
          {number, title, detail} <- [
            {"1", "Organize fragmented evidence",
             "AI agents gather sourced statements and connect proposals with attributable positions that people can inspect and help verify."},
            {"2", "Enable scrutiny and informed participation",
             "Let people explore different views, challenge interpretations, vote, and delegate to people they trust."},
            {"3", "Improve public debate and help trusted voices emerge",
             "Help creators understand their audiences and give thoughtful contributors opportunities to earn trust and visibility."},
            {"4", "Inform decisions and strengthen accountability",
             "Help decision-makers understand the evidence, competing proposals, and the people affected by their choices."},
            {"5", "Improve democratic outcomes",
             "Test whether better debate, broader participation, and more informed decisions lead to effective policies, starting with AI safety, jobs, and shared benefits."}
          ]
        }
        class="flex gap-4 rounded-xl border border-gray-200 p-5"
      >
        <span
          aria-hidden="true"
          class="flex h-8 w-8 shrink-0 items-center justify-center rounded-full bg-indigo-100 font-bold text-indigo-700"
        >
          {number}
        </span>
        <div>
          <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">{title}</h3>
          <p class="mt-1 leading-7 text-gray-600">{detail}</p>
        </div>
      </li>
    </ol>
    <p class="mt-5 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      Progress between these steps is not automatic. Accurate records need relevant users; informed participation needs useful ways to handle disagreement; institutional action needs incentives, authority, and implementation. New evidence and experience should also feed back into the record.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="change-evidence" class="border-t border-gray-200 py-8">
    <h2 id="change-evidence" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
      1. Make evidence easier to inspect
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      AI agents gather sourced statements to help people understand the landscape around a proposal without separately reconstructing hundreds of sources. Citizens, creators, researchers, journalists, and decision-makers can inspect the same record. Keeping positions attributable makes it possible to check their context, challenge an interpretation, and contribute missing evidence.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      Provenance is a starting point, not a guarantee of truth. A quote can be authentic while its claim is wrong. Coverage can also be incomplete or skewed. Scrutiny must extend to the selection of sources and the way proposals are framed.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="change-participation" class="border-t border-gray-200 py-8">
    <h2 id="change-participation" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
      2. Make disagreement more informative
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      A single summary can hide important differences. People might disagree about the probability of a dangerous capability, agree about the danger but disagree about an intervention, or reason from different empirical assumptions and values.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      Participation gives people ways to respond to the record. Opinions, votes, delegations, and changes of position can provide clues about reasoning. They do not reveal reasoning on their own: a changed vote might reflect new evidence, social influence, or a different interpretation of the question.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-5 rounded-xl bg-indigo-50 p-5">
      <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">
        The goal is better judgment, not simply greater agreement.
      </h3>
      <p class="mt-2 leading-7 text-gray-700">
        Popularity does not establish truth, and participants are not necessarily representative of the public or of experts. We want to learn which objections survive scrutiny, which evidence changes minds, and whether well-supported minority arguments receive attention.
      </p>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-7 text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">
      Delegation when nobody can evaluate everything
    </h3>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      AI governance spans machine learning, cybersecurity, biology, law, economics, and international relations. YouCongress already lets people delegate to trusted people while retaining the ability to vote directly. Today those delegations apply across issues.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      A future version could make trust specific to a domain and connect it more explicitly to evidence: whom do you rely on, for which questions, and why do they support an intervention? That is a direction to investigate, not an existing domain-specific delegation feature.
    </p>
    <h3 class="mt-7 text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">Help creators understand their audiences</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      Creators can use
      <.link href={~p"/reconsider"} class="font-medium text-indigo-700 underline">
        Reconsider
      </.link>
      to pair an article or video with relevant proposals and ask readers or viewers where they stood before and where they stand now. The results show reported changes in views and which featured people participants chose as delegates. That feedback could help creators identify disagreements and questions to explore next.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      These responses come from people who choose to participate. They do not represent the whole audience or establish that the content caused a change in opinion.
    </p>
    <h3 class="mt-7 text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">Help new trusted voices emerge</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      Sharing thoughtful arguments, responding to objections, and earning others’ trust through delegation could help contributors become known beyond their existing circles. We hope this creates opportunities for people who have never considered running for office to discover that others value their judgment. Public service is one possible outcome; contributing to better debate is valuable in itself.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      This is a hypothesis to test. Gaining delegates can indicate trust, but does not by itself establish sound judgment or readiness for office. We need to learn whether participation helps overlooked voices earn attention and whether that trust is well placed.
    </p>
    <aside class="mt-6 border-l-2 border-indigo-300 pl-5 text-sm leading-6 text-gray-600">
      <p>
        The Future of Life Foundation’s competition judges highlighted YouCongress’s interactive participation layer as an important area to explore, while noting that the entry was not a full epistemic stack. We take this as encouragement to test the approach, not proof of its impact. <a
          href="https://www.oliversourbut.net/p/flfs-epistemic-case-study-competition"
          class="font-medium text-indigo-700 underline"
        >Read the judges’ assessment</a>.
      </p>
    </aside>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="change-action" class="border-t border-gray-200 py-8">
    <h2 id="change-action" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
      3. Help decision-makers turn understanding into action
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      Decision-makers in government, public institutions, and other organizations could use YouCongress to compare sourced positions, examine objections, and understand what participants support and why. We aim to help them assess proposals alongside the perspectives of people affected by their choices. Participation can inform a decision, while decision-makers remain responsible for seeking missing perspectives and judging the evidence.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      Meaningful agreement around a concrete proposal could make cooperation easier, especially when the strongest unresolved objections are visible too. This could concern a safety requirement, support for workers affected by AI, or how economic benefits are shared. But revealing agreement is not the same as changing incentives or securing enforceable commitments.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-5 rounded-xl bg-gray-50 p-6">
      <h3 class="font-bold text-gray-900">One pathway: reducing catastrophic AI risk</h3>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-gray-600">
        A policy researcher examining
        <.link
          href={~p"/p/ai-labs-independent-evaluators-employee-like-access"}
          class="font-medium text-indigo-700 underline"
        >
          independent access to AI labs
        </.link>
        could use YouCongress to locate sourced positions, investigate objections, and develop a better briefing. That briefing might inform a consultation, an evaluation policy, or an institutional decision.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-gray-600">
        This depends on credibility, relevance, timing, and access to the decision process. Adoption would still need to be followed by implementation and evidence that the safeguard works.
      </p>
    </div>
    <h3 class="mt-7 text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">
      A longer-term direction: accountability over time
    </h3>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      We would like to make it easier to trace how institutional commitments change, whether promised evaluations happen, and how experts update their positions. Dated sources are a foundation; systematic commitment tracking and compliance assessment require further work.
    </p>
    <h3 class="mt-7 text-lg font-bold text-gray-900">Explore the conditions for cooperation</h3>
    <p class="mt-3 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      Another possibility is conditional commitments: “I would support slowing frontier AI development if other major actors agreed and compliance could be independently verified.” Exploring those conditions could reveal possible agreements that unconditional votes miss. YouCongress does not currently offer infrastructure to make or enforce these commitments.
    </p>
    <.link
      href={~p"/moloch"}
      class="mt-4 inline-block font-semibold text-indigo-700 underline underline-offset-4"
    >
      Why coordination can fail, even with good intentions →
    </.link>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="change-status" class="border-t border-gray-200 py-8">
    <h2 id="change-status" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
      What exists, and what we want to investigate
    </h2>
    <div class="mt-6 grid gap-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-gray-200 p-6">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-indigo-900">Current foundation</h3>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 leading-7 text-gray-600">
          <li>Sourced positions and verification workflows.</li>
          <li>Direct voting and global delegation.</li>
          <li>
            <.link href={~p"/reconsider"} class="text-indigo-700 underline">Reconsider</.link>: audience feedback through reported before-and-after views and delegate selections, currently in beta.
          </li>
          <li>Source records and dated positions.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="rounded-xl border border-indigo-200 bg-indigo-50 p-6">
        <h3 class="font-bold text-indigo-900">Research and future directions</h3>
        <ul class="mt-4 list-disc space-y-3 pl-5 leading-7 text-gray-600">
          <li>Map assumptions and unresolved objections more systematically.</li>
          <li>Explore trust specific to a domain.</li>
          <li>Investigate which evidence explains changes in views.</li>
          <li>
            Test whether participation helps overlooked contributors earn well-founded trust.
          </li>
          <li>Track institutional commitments and follow-through.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section aria-labelledby="change-tests" class="border-t border-gray-200 py-8">
    <h2 id="change-tests" class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900">
      How would we know this is working?
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      We want to test the links in this theory, not assume them. Useful evaluations would ask:
    </p>
    <ul class="mt-5 list-disc space-y-4 pl-5 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      <li>
        Can researchers reconstruct a debate faster, compared with their usual methods, without losing accuracy or important minority arguments?
      </li>
      <li>
        Can participants explain opposing arguments and identify the actual points of disagreement more accurately?
      </li>
      <li>
        Do creators learn something useful about their audiences’ views and use it to address substantive questions or objections?
      </li>
      <li>
        Do previously overlooked contributors earn trust through useful arguments and engagement, and does this open opportunities for public service?
      </li>
      <li>
        Do corrections and new evidence improve the record, including its coverage of missing perspectives?
      </li>
      <li>
        Do decision-makers use the resource in identifiable briefings, consultations, evaluations, or decisions, and does it help them consider evidence and affected people they would otherwise miss?
      </li>
      <li>
        Where AI policies change, is there evidence of a contribution from YouCongress—and of effective implementation that improves safety, outcomes for workers, or how benefits are shared?
      </li>
    </ul>
    <p class="mt-5 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      More votes, greater agreement, or more time on site would not by themselves demonstrate better judgment. Apparent consensus can reflect missing voices, shared errors, or social pressure. Evidence of downstream impact will be harder to establish than product usage.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-gray-600">
      We intend to publish what we learn, including evidence that parts of this approach do not work, and revise the theory accordingly.
    </p>
  </section>

  <footer class="mt-3 rounded-2xl bg-indigo-950 p-6 text-white sm:p-8">
    <p class="text-xl font-semibold leading-8">
      Our long-term aim is a democracy with better public debate, more informed decisions, and more opportunities for trusted voices to emerge.
    </p>
    <p class="mt-4 leading-7 text-indigo-100">
      Help us examine both the proposals and the assumptions behind this approach.
    </p>
    <div class="mt-6 flex flex-wrap gap-5 font-semibold">
      <.link href={~p"/explore"} class="underline underline-offset-4">Explore proposals</.link>
      <.link href={~p"/contact"} class="underline underline-offset-4">
        Discuss a research collaboration
      </.link>
      <.link href={~p"/about"} class="underline underline-offset-4">About YouCongress</.link>
    </div>
  </footer>
</article>
